# SHA-256 of names that must never appear in any committed file.
#
# Stored as hashes, not plaintext, for the reason the list exists: the names
# are private, so writing them here would be the very disclosure the check
# prevents. A hash commits to a name without revealing it, which is what lets
# this ship in a public repository with no secret, no environment variable and
# no untracked local file a fresh clone would be missing.
#
# Check C6 tokenises every file git knows about — tracked OR present and not
# ignored — and hashes each token. A match fails the build and prints the FILE,
# never the name: a public CI log is published output.
#
# What the tokeniser reaches, so that adding a name here is not guesswork. Any
# word of two characters or more, whole hyphenated and underscored runs, and
# adjacent words re-joined with `-`, `_` and nothing — so an entry for
# `some-name` also catches `some name` written in a sentence. It cannot split a
# run of letters back into words, because doing so would require knowing the
# name, which is the one thing this file is built not to hold.
#
# Threat model, stated honestly: this stops an accidental mention by an agent
# or a contributor. It does not stop someone who already knows the name from
# confirming it by hashing a guess. That is acceptable; guarding against an
# adversary who already has the secret is not what this is for.
#
# To add a name, without ever writing it down:
#   printf '%s' 'the-name' | sha256sum | cut -d' ' -f1 >> docs/governance/private-names.sha256
# Add one line per spelling that cannot be derived from another: hyphenated,
# underscored, and concatenated. The spaced form needs no line of its own.
26eb5588ac893b8f2a412ee324189c219bae05c478cb065a93dd40757fece44d
bb72081bb91acded5c799abb2c8e2476e5c9e59bd27cea41f7c87064a248bc96
019f6bced9819b878a4c1576dfd8f04a0aba0458fac348f38136e921b1f9535c
